\begin{abstract}
Federated sequence $k$-nearest-neighbor search incurs substantial local edit-distance computation. Distribution-aware allocation reduces requested candidate counts, yet composition-only bounds can retain many order-dissimilar strings, and a positive quota at every provider can trigger unnecessary local search. We propose FedSeq, a sequence-oriented query algorithm that couples an order-aware lower bound with frontier-certified zero allocation and an adaptive fallback. A bit-parallel longest-common-subsequence filter strengthens the count bound, while a broker-side frontier certificate identifies providers that can safely return no additional real candidates. When no initial cold provider occurs, FedSeq retains the distribution-aware allocation path. We present the complete query procedure and prove its lower-bound admissibility and exact retrieval correctness. FedSeq$^*$ adds shifted two-sided geometric quota perturbation with zero-permitting requests; we derive a conditional sensitivity bound and calibrate both retrieval rounds to a composed quota budget. Experiments compare five algorithms on synthetic sequences and a real Rfam-derived collection under IID and Non-IID partitions, vary retrieval and privacy parameters, and test graded heterogeneity, an independent edit generator, and unclustered controls. On the synthetic defaults, FedSeq reduces mean total query CPU by $\DefaultSpeedupMin$--$\DefaultSpeedupMax\times$ relative to DANN. Module ablations show complementary gains from order filtering and provider scheduling, and an encrypted TCP study further evaluates their computational benefit under local transport.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Federated similarity search, edit distance, lower bounds, provider scheduling, discrete noise.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
String comparisons are used to match duplicate records and retrieve similar sequences~\cite{duplicates,simstudy,topkedit,approxngram}. When collections reside at independent organizations, federated $k$-nearest-neighbor ($k$NN) search combines local results without centralizing the complete datasets. For unit-cost edit distance, local verification requires dynamic programming over sequence pairs. Verification can dominate query cost.

DANN in FedKNN allocates local retrieval quotas in two rounds using lower-bound candidate summaries~\cite{fedknn}. This reduces unnecessary local top-$k$ requests when answers concentrate at a few providers. We study how sequence order can improve these bounds and allocation decisions.

\input{figures/motivation}

Figure~\ref{fig:motivation} illustrates two challenges. \emph{Order ambiguity} occurs when strings have similar composition but different symbol order. For example, \texttt{AABB} and \texttt{BBAA} have identical character histograms, so their character-count bound is zero, although their exact edit distance is four. Such candidates can consume local verification and distort the distribution inferred by the broker. \emph{Forced participation} occurs when every provider receives a positive quota despite strong evidence that some providers cannot contribute to the global answer. Finding even one local neighbor may require scanning many strings; requesting one result is not equivalent to computing one distance.

These challenges interact. Stronger local bounds improve both candidate filtering and the broker's evidence about providers. However, a zero origin count in the preliminary global lower-bound top-$k$ is not sufficient to exclude a provider: optimistic bounds at other providers may later be contradicted by exact distances. We therefore allow zero requests, but revisit a provider unless the returned evidence justifies omitting it.

FedSeq addresses this interaction through three cooperating modules. First, an order-aware filter combines the character-count bound with the classical LCS bound, using a bit-parallel scan to evaluate it efficiently. Second, a certified cold-provider rule compares real returned candidates with the minimum lower-bound key of each unsent local suffix. The broker derives a sufficient second-round prefix length, including zero when $k$ real candidates precede every unsent record. Third, an adaptive fallback retains DANN scheduling when every provider has a positive initial origin count. Order filtering removes local candidates; the certificate removes unnecessary provider searches. Both retain exact unperturbed answers.

Perturbing the first quota creates a further refinement problem: the returned prefix can differ from the clean allocation map. FedSeq$^*$ applies the frontier rule to that actual prefix, with shifted two-sided geometric requests and fixed-length dummy replies. We analyze the observer, neighboring relation, conditional sensitivity, and two-round composition. The LCS bound and geometric distribution are established techniques~\cite{allison,geometric}; our contribution is their use with sufficient zero allocation, a refinement rule valid after arbitrary first requests, and calibrated quota release. The noisy variant avoids switching on unperturbed zero counts.

\section{System Model and Preliminaries}
\subsection{System Model}
Figure~\ref{fig:system} separates three roles. Each provider $P_i$ owns its records and evaluates local bounds and exact prefixes. The broker merges summaries, allocates two rounds of retrieval, and selects the final neighbors. An authorized user submits $(q,k)$ and receives the result. The logical privacy boundary encloses intermediate summaries, candidates and frontiers; the quota observer sees only the released request vectors analyzed in Section~\ref{sec:noise}.
\input{figures/system_model}
\subsection{Federated Sequence Retrieval}
Let $P_1,\ldots,P_m$ hold disjoint record sets $D_1,\ldots,D_m$, and let $D=\bigcup_iD_i$, $N=|D|$. A query is a byte string $q$, and $1\leq k\leq N$. The distance $\ED(x,q)$ is unit-cost Levenshtein distance: insertion, deletion, and substitution each cost one. A conventional dynamic-programming implementation evaluates a pair in $O(|x||q|)$ time~\cite{wagner}. All compared methods share this exact kernel.

Each record has a provider identifier $i$ and a persistent local identifier $\operatorname{id}(x)$. We define its exact key
\begin{equation}
 K(x)=(\ED(x,q),i,\operatorname{id}(x)),
\end{equation}
ordered lexicographically. The exact answer consists of the smallest $k$ keys in $D$. Persistent identifiers distinguish records with equal content. This stable order also makes correctness checks reproducible when several candidates tie at the boundary.

A local lower-bound function $\ell$ is admissible if $\ell(x,q)\leq\ED(x,q)$ for every pair. Its key is $L(x)=(\ell(x,q),i,\operatorname{id}(x))$, so $L(x)\preceq K(x)$. Each provider supports two operations: $\mathsf{LocalKLB}(k)$ returns its smallest $k$ lower-bound keys, or all records if fewer exist; $\mathsf{LocalKNN}(t)$ returns its smallest $\min(t,|D_i|)$ exact keys, with an empty result for $t=0$.

The local search backend in this study computes and sorts lower-bound keys, then scans them while maintaining a local top-$t$ set. Once the set is full and the next bound key cannot precede its largest exact key, the remaining records are pruned. Exact distances are cached within a query and shared across its two retrieval rounds. Algorithm~\ref{alg:local} implements $\mathsf{LocalKNN}(t)$ and returns the full prefix, so the two replies may overlap. A second request can therefore extend a local prefix without recomputing distances already evaluated. The cache is reset for each measured algorithm run.

\subsection{Execution and Observation Model}
Providers are honest and return exact local prefixes under admissible bounds. We minimize total query CPU, $T_{\mathrm{broker}}+\sum_iT_{P_i}$; response time is evaluated separately because it also depends on communication rounds.

The quota observer sees requests and public parameters; summaries, candidates, frontiers, and branch state remain internal. Section~\ref{sec:noise} defines adjacency and the view. Wider trace protection additionally requires protected processing and a fixed schedule~\cite{fedknn}.

