\subsection{Order-Aware Local Search}
\label{sec:bound}
\subsubsection{Combined lower bound}
Gram signatures provide admissible filters~\cite{gravano,vgram,edjoin}. Let $h_g(x)$ be a fixed-width mapped $g$-gram count vector. One edit changes its $\ell_1$ difference by at most $2g$, giving
\begin{equation}
 \ell_C(x,q)=\max\left\{\big||x|-|q|\big|,
 \left\lceil\frac{\|h_g(x)-h_g(q)\|_1}{2g}\right\rceil\right\}.
 \label{eq:count}
\end{equation}
Mapping collisions weaken but do not invalidate this bound. We use the released MinSearch count-signature component with $g=1$ on a shared scan backend; its index and positional pipeline are excluded~\cite{minsearch}.

Let $\LCS(x,q)$ denote the length of a longest common subsequence, preserving order but not requiring contiguity. The order-aware component is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \ell_O(x,q)&=\max(|x|,|q|)-\LCS(x,q),\\
 \ell_F(x,q)&=\max\{\ell_C(x,q),\ell_O(x,q)\}.
\end{aligned}
 \label{eq:combined}
\end{equation}
\input{figures/order_example}
\textbf{Order-bound example.} For $x_4=\texttt{BBAA}$, the count bound is zero. Its longest common subsequence with $q$ has length 2, giving $\ell_F=2\leq\ED(x_4,q)=4$. Figure~\ref{fig:order-example} displays the matches and the tightened bound on a distance axis.

\begin{lemma}[Admissibility]
For unit-cost Levenshtein distance, $\ell_F(x,q)\leq\ED(x,q)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider an optimal alignment with $M$ exact character matches, $S$ substitutions, $I$ insertions, and $D'$ deletions. Its cost is $S+I+D'$. The matched characters form a common subsequence, so $\LCS(x,q)\geq M$. Since $|x|=M+S+D'$ and $|q|=M+S+I$,
\begin{align*}
 \max(|x|,|q|)-\LCS(x,q)
 &\leq S+\max(I,D')\\
 &\leq S+I+D'=\ED(x,q).
\end{align*}
The length and gram-count terms in (\ref{eq:count}) are also admissible, so their maximum with $\ell_O$ remains admissible.
\end{proof}

\subsubsection{Bit-parallel evaluation}
Our implementation uses the classical bit-string LCS approach~\cite{allison}. For a query of at most 128 bytes, it precomputes a 128-bit position mask $M_c$ for each byte value $c$. Given a bit state $S$, the scan over database characters updates
\begin{equation}
 X=M_c\lor S,\quad Y=(S\ll1)\lor1,\quad
 S=X\land\neg(X-Y).
 \label{eq:bits}
\end{equation}
The final population count of $S$ gives the LCS length. The code uses GCC/Clang's unsigned 128-bit type, and counts set bits in its two 64-bit halves. Query masks are reused across records. 

For queries of at most 128 bytes, the record scan takes $O(|x|)$ bit-word operations. Longer queries or unsupported compilers use the length-difference fallback; longer records remain supported against short queries.

The stopping comparison uses full stable keys, including persistent identities, and preserves the oracle's deterministic boundary order.

Every record receives a bound before retrieval, including at zero-quota providers. The order scan pays off only when saved exact comparisons exceed its cost. \textbf{Local-scan example.} At $P_1$, bound order is $x_1,x_2,x_3$. A target of two verifies $x_1$ and $x_2$, leaving threshold $K(x_2)=(1,1,x_2)$. The next bound $(1,1,x_3)$ follows it under stable identities, so $x_3$ needs no exact evaluation. Algorithm~\ref{alg:local} retains this prefix and its distance cache.

