\subsection{Adaptive Certified Cold-Provider Scheduling}
\label{sec:schedule}
\subsubsection{Initial allocation and fallback}
FedSeq merges summaries under $\ell_F$ and keeps raw origin counts $a_i$. If every $a_i>0$, it uses DANN allocation with the stronger bound. Otherwise it requests prefixes $a_i$ and applies frontier refinement to all providers (Figure~\ref{fig:cold-example}). A zero origin count permits initial omission, but does not certify permanent exclusion.

Local top-$k$ summaries suffice: an omitted local key has $k$ local predecessors and cannot enter global top-$k$. Hence $\sum_i a_i=k$ for $N\geq k$, and each $a_i$ is feasible.

Threshold aggregation~\cite{threshold,tput} combines scores for shared objects; here, frontiers certify records in disjoint collections.


\subsubsection{Frontier-based refinement}
Let $A_i=\mathsf{LocalKNN}(a_i)$ and $r_i=|A_i|$. From the exact first replies, the broker constructs
\begin{equation}
 R=\Top_k\left(\bigcup_i A_i\right).
\end{equation}
Each provider reports a frontier key
\begin{equation}
 F_i=\min\{L(x):x\in D_i\setminus A_i\},
 \label{eq:frontier}
\end{equation}
or a sentinel larger than every key if all its records have been sent. Admissibility implies $F_i\preceq K(x)$ for every unsent record $x$ at that provider. The frontier is computed from the sorted bound keys already available locally; it is included with the first reply, rather than requiring another logical retrieval phase.

Define two candidate counts:
\begin{align}
 b_i&=|\{y\in R:K(y)\prec F_i\}|,\\
 s_i&=\begin{cases}
 |\{y\in R:K(y)\preceq K(z_i)\}|,& A_i\neq\varnothing,\\
 0,& A_i=\varnothing,
 \end{cases}
\end{align}
where $z_i$ is the largest key in the exact prefix $A_i$. Every unsent local record follows $z_i$ strictly, so the $s_i$ candidates also precede every such record. Let
\begin{equation}
 B_i=\max(b_i,s_i),\qquad
 t_i=\min\{k,\;r_i+k-B_i\}.
 \label{eq:quota}
\end{equation}
The broker requests $\mathsf{LocalKNN}(t_i)$, retains the first-round candidates, and merges both rounds. Local top-$t_i$ requests are clipped to local collection size by the provider.

\textbf{Certificate example.} The running instance's global lower-bound top-two is $x_1,x_2$, so $(a_1,a_2,a_3)=(2,0,0)$. The exact first reply is $R=\{x_1,x_2\}$. Its two keys precede $F_2=(1,2,x_5)$ and $F_3=(2,3,x_9)$, hence $B_2=B_3=2$ and $t_2=t_3=0$. The final targets are $(2,0,0)$. With the count bound and positive floor, the first targets are $(2,1,1)$: local scans evaluate eight distances in this constructed instance, versus two for FedSeq. These are checked example counts, not benchmark measurements.

\subsubsection{Why a local quota can be expensive}
Even $t=1$ can inspect a whole shard when its bounds stay below its best exact distance. Yet the broker can omit that search if $k$ real global candidates already precede its unsent frontier.

\subsection{Correctness}
\begin{lemma}[Sufficient local prefix]
Suppose a provider has already returned its exact prefix of length $r_i$, and $B_i$ distinct real records are known to precede every unsent local record. Then retaining that prefix and retrieving a prefix of length $\min(k,r_i+k-B_i)$ includes every global top-$k$ record from that provider.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Order the unsent local records by their exact keys. The $j$th unsent record has the $B_i$ certified real records before it. None of those records is an unsent record from the same provider, since each certified key precedes the minimum admissible frontier or the last exact prefix key. The previous $j-1$ unsent local records are distinct additional predecessors. If $j>k-B_i$, the record therefore has at least $k$ predecessors and cannot be in the global top-$k$. It suffices to extend the local prefix by $k-B_i$ positions. No provider can contribute more than $k$ global records, which justifies the cap.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unperturbed FedSeq correctness]
Under exact local-prefix replies and admissible stable-key frontiers, FedSeq returns the exact global top-$k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In the frontier branch, both the $b_i$ set and the $s_i$ set consist of real records preceding every unsent local record. Select the larger set to obtain a valid certificate of size $B_i$. The preceding lemma proves that the merged replies contain every true global neighbor. Selecting their smallest $k$ keys is therefore exact.

In the fallback branch, every raw origin count is positive and the first prefix has length $a_i$. If its last key has rank $p_i$ in $R$, all unsent local records follow at least those $p_i$ real candidates; the same lemma gives $a_i+k-p_i$. If the last key is outside $R$, the $k$ keys in $R$ precede it and no additional local contribution is needed. The reference's $p_i=k$ convention retains the already returned prefix. Empty providers contribute no answer records. Thus the fallback is also correct.
\end{proof}

