\subsubsection{How the two pruning modules reinforce each other}
The combined bound also strengthens the frontier used by the broker. For fixed real candidates, we can state precisely how a stronger frontier changes the next quota.

\begin{proposition}[Bound-to-quota coupling]
\label{prop:coupling}
Fix a provider's first exact prefix $A_i$, the real set $R$, and their stable identities. Replacing $\ell_C$ by the pointwise stronger $\ell_F$ cannot increase the certified second-round target in (\ref{eq:quota}).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every unsent record satisfies $L_F(x)\succeq L_C(x)$; hence $F_i^F=\min_{x\notin A_i}L_F(x)\succeq F_i^C$. More real keys can precede the stronger frontier, so $b_i^F\geq b_i^C$. The exact-prefix predecessor count $s_i$ and prefix size $r_i$ are fixed. Therefore $\max(b_i^F,s_i)\geq\max(b_i^C,s_i)$, and applying the same quota formula yields $t_i^F\leq t_i^C$.
\end{proof}
The proposition fixes the returned evidence; complete executions can obtain different first prefixes. The ablation tests this bound--allocation interaction.

\subsection{Computational Cost}
For $n_i=|D_i|$, count filtering costs $O(N)$ at fixed signature dimension. With $|q|\leq128$, query masks cost $O(m|q|)$ and order scans cost $O(\sum_{x\in D}|x|)$ word operations; sorting costs $\sum_iO(n_i\log n_i)$. If $\mathcal V_i$ contains the distinct verified records, exact work is
\begin{equation*}
 \sum_i\sum_{x\in\mathcal V_i}O(|q||x|).
\end{equation*}
A small quota need not imply small $|\mathcal V_i|$. The cache prevents reevaluation between rounds; heap maintenance costs $O(H\log(k+1))$, with $H\leq2N$ visited entries. Frontiers scan sorted bounds past sent identifiers without new distances. Broker merges use at most $mk$ summary and $O(mk)$ real keys per round.

Order filtering and certification pay off when saved verification exceeds their scan and allocation overhead. Cold scheduling does not remove initial bound construction; the unstructured control tests the case where that added cost brings little pruning. Communication is separate: allocation retains a summary exchange and two retrieval phases. Clean replies carry real prefixes, while noisy replies occupy $k$ slots. CPU savings therefore need not remove round-trip boundaries or padded payloads; the transport experiment measures this distinction.

\section{\texorpdfstring{FedSeq$^*$}{FedSeq*} and Quota Privacy}
\label{sec:noise}
\subsection{Discrete Perturbation and Query Procedure}
FedSeq$^*$ uses the combined bound and the frontier allocation rule at every provider. Unlike the clean algorithm, it does not switch refinement rules according to raw zero counts: a neighboring record could change that switch and alter targets at many unchanged providers. Using one refinement rule makes the conditional sensitivity tractable while retaining the same frontier evidence. Algorithm~\ref{alg:star} specifies both rounds. The clean algorithm retains its adaptive DANN fallback.

For noise rate $\beta>0$, let $\alpha=\exp(-\beta)$ and draw independent $X,Y$ with $\Pr[X=t]=(1-\alpha)\alpha^t$, $t\geq0$. Their difference $Z=X-Y$ has
\begin{equation}
 \Pr[Z=z]=\frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha}\alpha^{|z|},\qquad
 \operatorname{Var}(Z)=\frac{2\alpha}{(1-\alpha)^2}.
\end{equation}
This is the established two-sided geometric distribution~\cite{geometric}. Privacy analysis assumes private independent draws. The revised experiments use private OpenSSL randomness for noise; public seeds control only populations, partitions, and measurement order. For a public padding-tail parameter $\lambda$, set
\begin{equation}
 \eta(\beta,\lambda)=\max\left\{0,\left\lceil\frac{-\ln(\lambda(1+\alpha))}{\beta}\right\rceil-1\right\}
 \label{eq:eta}
\end{equation}
and release the requested quota
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf{Perturb}(c;\beta,\lambda)=\clip_{[0,k]}(c+\eta+Z).
 \label{eq:geometric}
\end{equation}
Figure~\ref{fig:quota-noise} shows the perturbation and reply interface. The zero floor retains a chance of omitting real local retrieval. Replies contain $k$ slots, with unused positions filled by dummies. Our DANN$^*$ comparator retains shifted Laplace noise, upward rounding, and a positive quota floor, but is calibrated to the same joint quota budget as FedSeq$^*$ below. The ablation separates the bound, the floor change, and the integer-noise construction.
