\subsection{Neighboring Relation and Protected View}
We use record-replacement adjacency: $D\sim D'$ if one sequence changes at one fixed provider, with record identities and provider sizes unchanged. The query, $k$, $m$, provider sizes, stable identifiers, and noise parameters are public. A replacement does not move a record to another provider. The quota observer sees
\begin{equation}
 V(D)=(\widetilde{\boldsymbol c}^{(1)},\widetilde{\boldsymbol c}^{(2)}).
\end{equation}
The broker's exact candidates, raw origin counts, frontiers, and allocation branch remain internal to the protected logical computation. The exact answer is delivered to the authorized query user rather than released to this observer. This protects work allocation rather than the final answer~\cite{fedknn,do}.

\begin{definition}[Quota differential privacy]
The release $V$ is $(E,0)$-differentially private if for every $D\sim D'$ and every set $S$ of possible quota transcripts,
\begin{equation}
 \Pr[V(D)\in S]\leq \exp(E)\Pr[V(D')\in S].
\end{equation}
\end{definition}
For the second round, fix the first released vector $h$. Let $\boldsymbol f_1(D)$ be the clean first vector and $\boldsymbol f_2(D,h)$ the clean second vector obtained by executing the allocation procedure with first requests $h$. Define
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \Delta_1&=\sup_{D\sim D'}\|\boldsymbol f_1(D)-\boldsymbol f_1(D')\|_1,\\
 \Delta_2&=\sup_{h,D\sim D'}\|\boldsymbol f_2(D,h)-\boldsymbol f_2(D',h)\|_1.
\end{aligned}
 \label{eq:sens}
\end{equation}
Fixing $h$ is essential: the second request is adaptive, so its sensitivity must hold for every common released history~\cite{calibrate,dwork}.

\subsection{Sensitivity and Geometric-Mechanism Analysis}
\begin{lemma}[One-exchange stability]
\label{lem:exchange}
For fixed query, provider sizes, stable identifiers, and first requests $h$, record-replacement neighbors change the union of exact first prefixes by at most one removed key and one inserted key. Its global top-$k$ changes by at most one exchange. A predecessor count at an unchanged threshold therefore changes by at most one.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Only the replaced record's provider changes. The exact ordering of its other records is unchanged, and its prefix cardinality $\min(h_i,|D_i|)$ is fixed. Changing one key can replace at most one prefix member. All other prefixes are identical. Taking top-$k$ of sets differing by one exchange preserves this property: their common keys have the same order and can exchange at most one boundary member. For any unchanged threshold, at most one selected key leaves and at most one enters; their net effect on the predecessor count is at most one.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Conditional allocation sensitivity]
\label{lem:sensitivity}
The first vectors of FedSeq$^*$ and the positive-floor DANN$^*$ comparator satisfy $\Delta_1\leq2$. Algorithm~\ref{alg:star} satisfies $\Delta_2\leq k+m-1$. DANN$^*$ satisfies $\Delta_2\leq k+m$. Thus $k+m$ is a common second-round calibration bound.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Replacing a record changes only its lower-bound key. Merging local lower-bound top-$k$ summaries is equivalent to global lower-bound top-$k$. The selected set exchanges at most one key, so the origin vector changes by at most two in $\ell_1$. FedSeq$^*$ uses this vector directly. Applying $\max(a_i,1)$ coordinatewise for DANN$^*$ cannot increase its $\ell_1$ difference.

Fix a common first vector $h$, and let $j$ be the changed provider. For $i\neq j$, the prefix $A_i$, its last key $z_i$, frontier $F_i$, and size $r_i$ are unchanged. Lemma~\ref{lem:exchange} bounds the changes of $b_i$ and $s_i$ by one. The maximum $B_i=\max(b_i,s_i)$ is also changed by at most one, as is the clamped target $t_i=\min(k,r_i+k-B_i)$. At provider $j$, both targets lie in $[0,k]$, so their difference is at most $k$. Summing gives $\Delta_2\leq k+(m-1)$.

For DANN$^*$, write $c_i=a_i^++k-p_i$, where $a_i^+=\max(a_i,1)$ and $p_i$ is the last prefix key's global rank, clipped at $k$; an empty or unselected last key has $p_i=k$. For $i\neq j$, one exchange changes this clipped rank by at most one. At $j$, $p_j\in[1,k]$ changes by at most $k-1$. Finally, $\|\boldsymbol a^+(D)-\boldsymbol a^+(D')\|_1\leq2$. The triangle inequality yields $2+(m-1)+(k-1)=k+m$.
\end{proof}
These bounds require exact prefixes and fixed provider membership. Small-population enumeration and random neighboring histories check the implementation; the proof supplies the general guarantee.

\begin{lemma}[Scalar and vector geometric privacy]
With an ideal integer sampler, (\ref{eq:geometric}) applied to a scalar function of sensitivity $d$ is $(\beta d,0)$-DP. Independent coordinate draws applied to a vector of $\ell_1$ sensitivity $\Delta$ are $(\beta\Delta,0)$-DP.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For an unclamped integer output $u$, the scalar likelihood ratio is
\begin{equation*}
 \alpha^{|u-c(D)-\eta|-|u-c(D')-\eta|}
 \leq \exp(\beta|c(D)-c(D')|).
\end{equation*}
The triangle inequality bounds the exponent. Multiplying independent coordinate probabilities bounds a vector's ratio by $\exp(\beta\|\boldsymbol c(D)-\boldsymbol c(D')\|_1)$. Summing these pointwise inequalities over any output event gives DP. A public shift and clamp preserve the bound by post-processing~\cite{dwork}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Two-Round Composition and Budget Calibration}
\begin{theorem}[Adaptive quota privacy]
For Algorithm~\ref{alg:star}, let $\beta_1,\beta_2$ be public fixed rates, and let $\Delta_1,\Delta_2$ bound (\ref{eq:sens}). The protected joint quota view satisfies $(\beta_1\Delta_1+\beta_2\Delta_2,0)$-DP. Setting $\beta_r=\varepsilon_r/\Delta_r$ with $\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2=\varepsilon$ gives $(\varepsilon,0)$-DP for this view.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For a first vector $h$ and second vector $v$, factor the transcript probability as
\begin{equation*}
 \Pr[V(D)=(h,v)]=\Pr[M_1(D)=h]\Pr[M_2(D,h)=v].
\end{equation*}
The first ratio is at most $\exp(\beta_1\Delta_1)$. For the same fixed $h$, the second ratio is at most $\exp(\beta_2\Delta_2)$ by the conditional sensitivity bound. Their product is bounded by $\exp(\beta_1\Delta_1+\beta_2\Delta_2)$. Summing over transcript events proves the claim. Post-processing preserves this bound.
\end{proof}

All revised noisy experiments use equal half-budgets and the common calibration
\begin{equation}
 \beta_1=\frac{\varepsilon}{4},\qquad
 \beta_2=\frac{\varepsilon}{2(k+m)}.
 \label{eq:calibration}
\end{equation}
This gives $2\beta_1+(k+m)\beta_2=\varepsilon$ for both comparators. The same rates govern geometric FedSeq$^*$ and Laplace DANN$^*$; public shifts, rounding, and clipping are post-processing. We deliberately use the common $k+m$ bound rather than giving FedSeq$^*$ a slightly larger rate from $k+m-1$. Hence the plotted $\varepsilon$ is a composed per-query quota budget, not a nominal scalar setting.

The theorem assumes ideal private sampling. The benchmark uses OpenSSL bits and floating inverse sampling; repeated observed queries require additional composition.

\subsection{Padding Tail and Execution Interface}
For $\eta\geq0$, the shifted negative tail is
\begin{equation}
 \Pr[\eta+Z<0]=\frac{\alpha^{\eta+1}}{1+\alpha}\leq\lambda.
 \label{eq:tail}
\end{equation}
For a clean quota in $[0,k]$, this bounds the probability that its noisy request is smaller. It controls one release's underallocation tail, rather than guaranteeing an exact final answer. Across $2m$ releases, a valid per-release conditional bound permits a union bound of $2m\lambda$. This bound may be loose, and the experiments measure actual retrieval errors. For the positive-floor comparator, the tail applies to its effective target $\min(c_i,k)$ when a raw target exceeds $k$.

\begin{proposition}[Refinement after arbitrary first requests]
For any first request vector $h$, if every second request in Algorithm~\ref{alg:star} is at least its frontier target $c_i$, the retained answer is exact. With ideal shifted geometric sampling, its distance-top-$k$ error probability is at most $\min(1,m\lambda)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The sufficient-prefix lemma applies to the actual $A_i$ for any $h$. Second requests dominating its targets contain all required records. Conditional on $h$, each underallocation event has probability at most $\lambda$ by (\ref{eq:tail}); a union bound over the second round and averaging over $h$ prove the claim. First-round perturbation does not need a separate failure term.
\end{proof}

Quota privacy extends to an external transcript only if every additional observation is a data-independent function of the private quota view and public information. This requires a fixed contact schedule and protection of raw broker states and reply contents. Host-visible branches, memory accesses, timing, and plaintext frontiers require additional protection; fixed-length dummy replies alone address message cardinality. Differentially oblivious operators~\cite{do,adore} provide the relevant execution framework. Our theorem is therefore stated for the defined quota view, while the local benchmark and encrypted transport measure its algorithmic and communication costs.

For an initial clean quota $c=0$, a request remains zero exactly when $Z\leq-\eta$. Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 \Pr[\widetilde c=0\mid c=0]=\frac{\alpha^{\eta}}{1+\alpha}.
 \label{eq:zero-prob}
\end{equation}
At the revised first-round default $\varepsilon=1$, $\beta_1=.25$ and $\lambda=.05$, $\eta_1=9$, giving a zero-request probability near .0593. Six initially unselected providers therefore yield about 5.64 expected positive first requests. Figure~\ref{fig:quota-noise} makes the underlying trade-off explicit: a zero clamp permits omission, but positive padding deliberately favors larger requests.

As the noise rate decreases, both dispersion and the positive padding shift increase, making requests more likely to reach $k$. Larger requests add few distance calculations at a provider whose scan is already cached. They add much more work when the clean query would stop early.

