\section{Related Work}
\textbf{String filters and search structures.} Approximate joins use grams~\cite{gravano,vgram}, prefix constraints~\cite{edjoin,pivotal}, partitions~\cite{passjoin,partitiontods,vchunk}, and asymmetric signatures~\cite{asymmetric}. Trie-Join shares prefixes~\cite{triejoin}, while AdaptJoin chooses them by cost~\cite{adaptjoin}.

Top-$k$ edit search~\cite{topkedit}, approximate grams~\cite{approxngram}, MinSearch~\cite{minsearch}, and minIL~\cite{minil} optimize sequence search. Pigeonring adds cumulative constraints~\cite{pigeonring}; Fast separates reductions from planning~\cite{fastsim}; comparative studies show workload dependence~\cite{simstudy}. Classical LCS bit strings~\cite{allison} supply our filter, distinct from exact edit-distance verification~\cite{myers,wagner}. FedSeq reuses LCS to strengthen both local filtering and the unsent frontier. This connects candidate rejection to provider-level request allocation; the shared scan backend isolates these effects from index design.

\textbf{Distributed and protected queries.} MassJoin~\cite{massjoin} and Dima~\cite{dima} combine distributed processing with signatures/indexes. Threshold aggregation~\cite{threshold,tput} ranks combined scores, whereas our records reside in disjoint collections. FedSeq extends FedKNN~\cite{fedknn} with frontier-certified zero quotas. Real predecessors, rather than an empty initial origin count, certify omission. The clean fallback retains DANN allocation when every provider has a positive origin count.

Secure collaborative execution includes SMCQL~\cite{smcql}, Conclave~\cite{conclave}, Senate~\cite{senate}, Secrecy~\cite{secrecy}, and spatial federation Hu-Fu~\cite{hufu}. CryptDB~\cite{cryptdb} and SDB~\cite{sdb} support encrypted operations; Opaque~\cite{opaque} and ObliDB~\cite{oblidb} provide trusted/oblivious execution needed around our protected quota view.

\textbf{Privacy of observable work.} Sensitivity, composition, and post-processing~\cite{calibrate,dwork} govern our adaptive quota release; geometric noise is established~\cite{geometric}. DJoin~\cite{djoin} and Shrinkwrap~\cite{shrinkwrap} protect distributed queries and intermediate cardinalities. Differentially oblivious execution~\cite{do,adore} protects wider traces. Our analysis concerns a particular adaptive release: the second target depends on exact prefixes retrieved under the first noisy requests. Fixing that released history yields the conditional sensitivity needed to compose both rounds under one query budget.

\section{Conclusion}
FedSeq combines order-aware bounds with frontier-certified zero quotas. Its bound improves candidate rejection and supplies evidence for omitting exact retrieval at cold providers. FedSeq$^*$ applies geometric perturbation under a composed quota budget, supported by conditional sensitivity analysis.

Experiments on two controlled populations and minimally processed Rfam separate filtering gains from the additional, ownership-dependent benefit of cold scheduling. Encrypted transport shows why saved CPU need not yield equal response-time gains when retrieval rounds remain fixed. These findings support coupling sequence evidence with request scheduling. Future work will examine longer queries and integration with indexed local retrieval. Such integration must preserve exact prefixes and admissible frontiers to retain the same correctness argument.

