\subsection{Local Encrypted Transport}
\label{sec:transport}
We run all three datasets under IID and Non-IID ownership: 50 queries, three repeats, and five methods yield 4,500 runs. The four synthetic settings retain their original measurements; both Rfam transport cases use the same minimally processed records, queries, and default assignments as its native sweeps. One broker and concurrent provider processes exchange real TCP loopback messages, encrypted using AES-128-GCM with per-direction keys and non-repeating nonces. The delay model adds RTT/2 and a payload-size/bandwidth term; it does not measure WAN congestion.

For DNA Non-IID, Baseline, DANN, and FedSeq consume 220.65, 220.76, and 20.25 ms of CPU, while their mean response times are 102.55, 234.40, and 202.98 ms. Baseline uses one retrieval phase; the allocation methods retain a summary and two retrieval phases. Omitting exact local search reduces computation while leaving these round-trip boundaries in place.

Rfam CPU ratios are 5.82$\times$ (IID) and 6.45$\times$ (Non-IID); corresponding DANN/FedSeq response times are 245.19/206.48 and 267.49/207.12 ms. Fixed rounds limit the latency benefit. All 900 unperturbed Rfam runs are exact; 36 noisy strict mismatches (18 distance errors) remain included.

CPU includes broker/provider serialization and encryption but excludes blocking waits; wall time follows concurrent providers and communication. A separate SDK simulation probe checked the sorting ECALL interface. Retrieval runs outside hardware SGX, so these measurements characterize local transport under the stated quota model.

